# XSH fchmod()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.

assertion  XSH.fchmod.001
anchor     functions/fchmod.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       fchmod(fd, m) on a descriptor open on a regular file returns 0 and
           leaves the bits exactly m, for m = 0600, 0777 and 0 (the umask
           does not apply); it also
           works on a descriptor open on a directory.
test       t/001-sets-bits.c

assertion  XSH.fchmod.002
anchor     functions/fchmod.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       fchmod(fd, 0640) of an open file whose only name was removed
           succeeds, and fstat() reports the new bits.
test       t/002-unlinked.c

assertion  XSH.fchmod.003
anchor     functions/chmod.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      1988
says       After fchmod(), fstat() reports a later st_ctim (the test changes the
           mode again every 10 ms for up to 3 s until it moves).
timeout    20
test       t/003-ctime.c

assertion  XSH.fchmod.004
anchor     functions/fchmod.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      1988
says       fchmod() of a descriptor that is not open fails with EBADF.
test       t/004-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.fchmod.005
anchor     functions/fchmod.html#ERRORS/EPERM
class      required
since      1988
says       For a process without appropriate privileges, fchmod() of a file
           it does not own (mode 0666) fails with EPERM, both through a
           descriptor it opened for reading and writing and through one the
           privileged parent opened; the mode is unchanged.
needs      root
test       t/005-eperm.c

assertion  XSH.fchmod.006
anchor     functions/fchmod.html#DESCRIPTION
class      conditional
when       SHM
since      2001
says       fchmod(fd, 0600) of a shared memory object (created 0640) succeeds
           and fstat() then reports read and write bits 0600.
test       t/006-shm.c

assertion  XSH.fchmod.007
anchor     functions/fchmod.html#ERRORS
class      required
since      2008
says       fchmod(fd, 0750), with fd opened on a directory with O_SEARCH (an
           open descriptor), succeeds and sets the directory's bits.
note       O_SEARCH itself is a header item (Tier 0); without it this
           test is UNRESOLVED.
test       t/007-search-fd.c
